WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.539
This is the executive briefing for Book
15 of the Federal Modernization Workbook:

2
00:00:05.539 --> 00:00:08.525
Compliance Automation
and Day-2 Operations.

3
00:00:08.525 --> 00:00:12.606
It's for the people who own what
happens after cutover: the CIO,

4
00:00:12.606 --> 00:00:16.687
service owners, help desk and
operations leads, and the CISO.

5
00:00:16.687 --> 00:00:18.488
The book's argument is simple.

6
00:00:18.488 --> 00:00:22.488
A modernized estate is judged
on day two, at the help desk.

7
00:00:22.488 --> 00:00:25.568
Every routine change
there touches a control,

8
00:00:25.568 --> 00:00:29.863
so compliance lives or dies in
daily operations, not at the audit.

9
00:00:29.863 --> 00:00:34.515
I'll take four parts: why day two
decides, what changes after cutover,

10
00:00:34.515 --> 00:00:37.475
who runs it, and how you
prove and measure it.

11
00:00:37.475 --> 00:00:41.875
Each decision slide names the decision,
its owner, and when it's needed.

12
00:00:41.875 --> 00:00:43.840
Let's start on Monday morning.

13
00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:47.240
Cutover proves the new
estate can be built.

14
00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:50.928
The first locked-out user on Monday
proves whether it can be run.

15
00:00:50.928 --> 00:00:54.803
A user changed phones; a new
hire needs an account by nine.

16
00:00:54.803 --> 00:00:56.577
Each lands at the help desk.

17
00:00:56.577 --> 00:01:00.351
If the new model can't handle it
safely, the desk finds a way:

18
00:01:00.351 --> 00:01:03.564
an emergency admin account
that never goes away,

19
00:01:03.564 --> 00:01:08.864
an email treated as the request, a temporary
password sent back to the same inbox.

20
00:01:08.864 --> 00:01:11.303
Each shortcut is reasonable on the day.

21
00:01:11.303 --> 00:01:14.277
Together they rebuild the
estate you just replaced.

22
00:01:14.277 --> 00:01:18.977
The decision for the CIO: call a
wave complete only after a reset,

23
00:01:18.977 --> 00:01:23.315
an unlock, a joiner and a leaver
have run under the new model.

24
00:01:23.315 --> 00:01:25.780
Next, the riskiest of those.

25
00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:31.380
The most dangerous routine action on the
desk is resetting someone's sign-in method.

26
00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:35.605
After that reset, whoever registers
next controls the account.

27
00:01:35.605 --> 00:01:39.440
So a reset done on an unverified
request isn't support;

28
00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:42.142
it hands the account to whoever asked.

29
00:01:42.142 --> 00:01:48.240
Federal advisories describe callers posing
as employees to get exactly these resets,

30
00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.204
and OMB calls recovery a potential bypass.

31
00:01:51.204 --> 00:01:54.030
The program's answer is
a verification ladder.

32
00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:57.605
Someone who's lost everything
is identity-proofed first.

33
00:01:57.605 --> 00:02:01.730
Email, caller ID and personal
questions verify no one.

34
00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:04.529
The decision for the
CISO: own that ladder,

35
00:02:04.529 --> 00:02:09.168
put the most friction on privileged
accounts, and alert on every reset.

36
00:02:09.168 --> 00:02:11.490
Next, why volume matters.

37
00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:16.369
The day after go-live, the help
desk resets hundreds of passwords,

38
00:02:16.369 --> 00:02:19.953
onboards a dozen people and
changes group memberships.

39
00:02:19.953 --> 00:02:22.453
Every one of those
changes touches a control.

40
00:02:22.453 --> 00:02:26.427
Done through a governed path, the
authorization package stays true.

41
00:02:26.427 --> 00:02:29.691
Done as portal clicks, it
ages with every click.

42
00:02:29.691 --> 00:02:33.194
The case for automation is
volume: at day-two scale,

43
00:02:33.194 --> 00:02:37.552
records assembled by hand are
reconstructions, not evidence.

44
00:02:37.552 --> 00:02:41.472
A control that's designed and
deployed but not operated,

45
00:02:41.472 --> 00:02:45.391
with an owner, a gate, a clock
and an evidence feed, is a hope.

46
00:02:45.391 --> 00:02:49.728
The decision for the CIO and
sponsor: fund that operating layer,

47
00:02:49.728 --> 00:02:52.565
and it's mostly automation, not headcount.

48
00:02:52.565 --> 00:02:54.110
Next, what to buy.

49
00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:57.898
The program asks for a
contract, not a product.

50
00:02:57.898 --> 00:02:59.722
Every request gets an owner.

51
00:02:59.722 --> 00:03:04.160
The approval is recorded before the
change, and never by the requester.

52
00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:08.998
The platform acts through a least-privilege
identity, never standing admin rights.

53
00:03:08.998 --> 00:03:10.960
Every step emits evidence.

54
00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:15.040
And the platform's inventory
reconciles to the authoritative one,

55
00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:16.598
never the other way round.

56
00:03:16.598 --> 00:03:19.673
ServiceNow is the reference,
not a requirement.

57
00:03:19.673 --> 00:03:23.748
Its government cloud is certified at
the high level, which serves moderate

58
00:03:23.748 --> 00:03:27.823
systems conservatively, but record
that choice in your own authorization.

59
00:03:27.823 --> 00:03:32.185
The decision for the CIO: hold
any platform to that contract.

60
00:03:32.185 --> 00:03:34.280
Next, how work comes in.

61
00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:37.280
The operating rule is one front door.

62
00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:40.092
Nothing is handled as an
email to the cloud team.

63
00:03:40.092 --> 00:03:43.778
Portal requests, emails,
identity governance tasks

64
00:03:43.778 --> 00:03:47.379
and monitoring alerts all
land in one triage queue.

65
00:03:47.379 --> 00:03:51.468
Triage asks one question: what
authority does this task need?

66
00:03:51.468 --> 00:03:54.192
That answer picks the
pipeline and the approver,

67
00:03:54.192 --> 00:03:58.005
from a simple fix up to governance
actions that are never automated.

68
00:03:58.005 --> 00:04:01.574
One small rule matters: if
an approver doesn't respond,

69
00:04:01.574 --> 00:04:05.143
the request is rejected or
cancelled, never approved.

70
00:04:05.143 --> 00:04:07.006
Silence can't grant access.

71
00:04:07.006 --> 00:04:11.639
The decision for the service desk
manager: close the side doors, route the

72
00:04:11.639 --> 00:04:16.505
team mailbox into triage, and count any
work done outside it as a measured gap.

73
00:04:16.505 --> 00:04:18.600
Next, what the desk may do.

74
00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:21.650
The help desk executes approved work.

75
00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:23.850
It never decides what it executes.

76
00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:26.446
Technicians hold no standing admin roles;

77
00:04:26.446 --> 00:04:29.488
they switch on a scoped role
for the ticket in hand.

78
00:04:29.488 --> 00:04:33.200
The book flags a trap:
capability isn't authority.

79
00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:36.481
Some roles a help desk
might hold can delete users

80
00:04:36.481 --> 00:04:39.762
or approve app access, which
the desk must never do.

81
00:04:39.762 --> 00:04:44.462
Executive accounts sit in a protected
unit the general desk can't touch.

82
00:04:44.462 --> 00:04:48.950
And security monitoring flags any
elevation with no matching ticket.

83
00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:51.923
The decision for the
identity governance officer:

84
00:04:51.923 --> 00:04:56.312
scope the desk's privileged group, and
review its membership every quarter.

85
00:04:56.312 --> 00:04:59.010
Next, the tasks that need more.

86
00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:04.885
Some tasks let one person take over an
identity or open data to the wrong people,

87
00:05:04.885 --> 00:05:07.960
and the platform can't stop
the misuse on its own.

88
00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:11.731
For those, the program requires
two approvers besides the

89
00:05:11.731 --> 00:05:15.660
person doing the work, both
recorded before anyone elevates.

90
00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:20.030
The list is short: resets on accounts
the desk can't normally touch,

91
00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:23.914
repairing a cloud account's
link to its on-premises record,

92
00:05:23.914 --> 00:05:27.960
restoring accounts outside the
HR process, and bulk changes.

93
00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:31.578
The second approver checks that
the requester was verified,

94
00:05:31.578 --> 00:05:34.110
the change is exact,
and the urgency is real.

95
00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:37.635
A caller pressing for speed
goes to the emergency process.

96
00:05:37.635 --> 00:05:41.260
The decision: never waive the
second approver for urgency.

97
00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:43.590
Next, third-party apps.

98
00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:46.741
Connecting a cloud app to your directory

99
00:05:46.741 --> 00:05:49.353
can quietly move data
outside your boundary.

100
00:05:49.353 --> 00:05:54.227
In the government cloud most agencies use,
nothing in the app gallery is marked.

101
00:05:54.227 --> 00:05:57.539
CISA's directive requires
that only administrators

102
00:05:57.539 --> 00:06:00.703
can approve app access, and
the program applies that.

103
00:06:00.703 --> 00:06:02.966
But that rule decides who approves.

104
00:06:02.966 --> 00:06:05.421
It says nothing about
whether the service on

105
00:06:05.421 --> 00:06:08.077
the other end is authorized
to hold federal data.

106
00:06:08.077 --> 00:06:13.291
A tenant can pass every consent check and
still send data to an uncertified service.

107
00:06:13.291 --> 00:06:15.343
Only an authorization verdict,

108
00:06:15.343 --> 00:06:19.053
recorded before anything is
configured, closes that gap.

109
00:06:19.053 --> 00:06:23.327
The decision for the identity governance
officer: record each service's

110
00:06:23.327 --> 00:06:27.103
authorization status first, and
stop the request if it's missing.

111
00:06:27.103 --> 00:06:29.180
Next, the inventory.

112
00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:33.775
The program's rule: discovery
fills the configuration database,

113
00:06:33.775 --> 00:06:38.418
but the authoritative naming and addressing
record decides what an asset is.

114
00:06:38.418 --> 00:06:42.954
When they disagree, the database is
wrong, and the gap is a finding.

115
00:06:42.954 --> 00:06:45.517
This matters for patching and evidence.

116
00:06:45.517 --> 00:06:49.767
A missing asset is never patched,
while coverage still looks complete.

117
00:06:49.767 --> 00:06:52.530
A duplicate lets the fix land on one copy.

118
00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:56.980
And a connector that quietly returns
half its records raises no error at all.

119
00:06:56.980 --> 00:07:02.121
The decision for system owners: authorize
or remove every unknown asset the

120
00:07:02.121 --> 00:07:07.180
reconciliation finds, and fix identity
at the source, never in the database.

121
00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:09.520
Next, control automation.

122
00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:15.370
Control automation runs one loop everywhere:
test the setting, spot the drift,

123
00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:19.275
raise a task against the asset,
fix it on the native platform,

124
00:07:19.275 --> 00:07:22.383
and close it only when a
re-test confirms the fix.

125
00:07:22.383 --> 00:07:27.048
The cloud tools already test most
settings; your job is to own the result,

126
00:07:27.048 --> 00:07:30.607
and the coordination platform
reads but never writes.

127
00:07:30.607 --> 00:07:33.069
The platforms differ in ways that matter.

128
00:07:33.069 --> 00:07:36.543
Some integrations aren't
available in Azure Government,

129
00:07:36.543 --> 00:07:39.646
one AWS compliance service
is in maintenance mode,

130
00:07:39.646 --> 00:07:43.857
and on your own VMware estate the
evidence is entirely yours to build.

131
00:07:43.857 --> 00:07:48.087
The decision for the CISO: keep
the platform's identity read-only,

132
00:07:48.087 --> 00:07:50.358
with every automated fix approved.

133
00:07:50.358 --> 00:07:52.210
Next, who runs it.

134
00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:57.598
Day two is run by the people who touch
the estate most and decide the least.

135
00:07:57.598 --> 00:08:01.323
So every decision has to sit with
someone who isn't at the keyboard.

136
00:08:01.323 --> 00:08:06.059
A desk that approves its own resets
has rebuilt the standing administrator.

137
00:08:06.059 --> 00:08:09.248
The book separates
deciding, doing and owning.

138
00:08:09.248 --> 00:08:10.722
The desk executes.

139
00:08:10.722 --> 00:08:15.597
Its supervisor approves only routine
credential work for ordinary users.

140
00:08:15.597 --> 00:08:20.304
Platform owners own the runbooks;
control owners own the approval gates;

141
00:08:20.304 --> 00:08:23.598
the platform team builds
both and decides neither.

142
00:08:23.598 --> 00:08:29.251
And because runbooks decay, the decision
for the CIO is to give every catalog item,

143
00:08:29.251 --> 00:08:33.048
runbook and workflow one
named owner and a review date.

144
00:08:33.048 --> 00:08:34.920
Next, measuring it.

145
00:08:35.670 --> 00:08:40.157
How well the desk is staffed shows up in
its numbers before it shows up in an audit.

146
00:08:40.157 --> 00:08:42.182
Each measure says something specific.

147
00:08:42.182 --> 00:08:45.120
Tickets past their service
level show capacity.

148
00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:48.795
A rising escalation share
shows gaps in the runbooks.

149
00:08:48.795 --> 00:08:52.470
Tickets sent back for missing
evidence show slipping discipline.

150
00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:55.214
Privileged elevations
with no matching ticket

151
00:08:55.214 --> 00:08:57.683
mean the queue and the
roles have come apart.

152
00:08:57.683 --> 00:09:00.620
And work done outside the
front door is a side door.

153
00:09:00.620 --> 00:09:05.607
Each problem has a different fix: more
people, a better runbook, or training.

154
00:09:05.607 --> 00:09:09.821
The platform produces the numbers,
so nobody reports on their own work.

155
00:09:09.821 --> 00:09:15.182
The decision for the CIO: staff and source
the desk from these measures, monthly.

156
00:09:15.182 --> 00:09:17.270
Next, the change board.

157
00:09:18.020 --> 00:09:21.558
The change advisory board
authorizes production changes.

158
00:09:21.558 --> 00:09:25.072
But two decisions aren't the
board's: letting a class of change

159
00:09:25.072 --> 00:09:28.869
run without a person, and whether
a change needs reauthorization.

160
00:09:28.869 --> 00:09:33.170
Those are risk decisions, and risk
belongs to the authorizing official.

161
00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:36.120
The quietest way separation
is lost is here.

162
00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:38.489
An approval rule is just configuration,

163
00:09:38.489 --> 00:09:41.720
and the team that runs the
platform can usually edit it.

164
00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:45.457
So the decision for the board:
any change to approval rules,

165
00:09:45.457 --> 00:09:49.195
routing or integrations is a
normal change it authorizes.

166
00:09:49.195 --> 00:09:51.657
Nobody changes the gate they pass through.

167
00:09:51.657 --> 00:09:54.870
Next, proving it to an
authorizing official.

168
00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:58.008
A closed ticket proves
someone did some work.

169
00:09:58.008 --> 00:10:00.485
An assessed control proves the work held,

170
00:10:00.485 --> 00:10:03.983
and only that belongs in front
of an authorizing official.

171
00:10:03.983 --> 00:10:08.820
The program's rule: if a system can
observe a control, a system proves it.

172
00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:12.942
People sign only for what no system
can see, like a policy approval

173
00:10:12.942 --> 00:10:16.758
or a physical inspection, and
the package says which is which.

174
00:10:16.758 --> 00:10:20.565
Day-two work feeds a loop:
map each control to its tests,

175
00:10:20.565 --> 00:10:25.583
test on a schedule, and turn every gap
into a tracked plan-of-action item.

176
00:10:25.583 --> 00:10:30.430
The decision for the system owner: close
those items only on a passing re-test,

177
00:10:30.430 --> 00:10:34.432
and escalate a slipped milestone
instead of quietly moving it.

178
00:10:34.432 --> 00:10:36.430
Next, build or buy.

179
00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:41.955
Before anyone builds a compliance application,
know what your platform already does.

180
00:10:41.955 --> 00:10:45.907
ServiceNow's own applications
already schedule control tests,

181
00:10:45.907 --> 00:10:50.529
walk the federal risk framework, show
every plan-of-action item in one place,

182
00:10:50.529 --> 00:10:54.630
and import and export the
machine-readable formats assessors use.

183
00:10:54.630 --> 00:10:59.724
Some are separate subscriptions, and
some don't reach every government cloud;

184
00:10:59.724 --> 00:11:02.192
these are October 2026 observations.

185
00:11:02.192 --> 00:11:05.620
What they don't do out of the
box is specific to this design:

186
00:11:05.620 --> 00:11:10.508
tie every finding to the authoritative
asset, refuse to close work without proof,

187
00:11:10.508 --> 00:11:13.717
and keep the control list in
step with the agency's map.

188
00:11:13.717 --> 00:11:15.386
The decision for the CIO:

189
00:11:15.386 --> 00:11:19.392
configure the native apps first,
and build only those gaps.

190
00:11:19.392 --> 00:11:22.330
Next, checking the automation itself.

191
00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:27.099
The most dangerous failure in
automated operations is a silent one:

192
00:11:27.099 --> 00:11:31.693
a log source that stops sending, or a
detection that quietly stopped matching.

193
00:11:31.693 --> 00:11:34.730
It carries false confidence
into an authorization.

194
00:11:34.730 --> 00:11:37.493
So the runbook checks
the automation itself.

195
00:11:37.493 --> 00:11:42.498
Every day, a completeness check confirms
each log source is still sending,

196
00:11:42.498 --> 00:11:45.206
and a dark source is treated as an outage.

197
00:11:45.206 --> 00:11:49.556
Every week, harmless test events
confirm the detections still fire,

198
00:11:49.556 --> 00:11:52.430
and every hand-closed
task gets a second look.

199
00:11:52.430 --> 00:11:56.130
And a check that stops running
looks exactly like one that passes.

200
00:11:56.130 --> 00:12:01.292
The decision for the ISSO: confirm
each month that every check ran.

201
00:12:01.292 --> 00:12:03.750
Now, the decisions together.

202
00:12:04.500 --> 00:12:08.487
So, the decisions together, and
none of them is a procurement.

203
00:12:08.487 --> 00:12:12.820
The CIO closes waves only after
the everyday tasks run cleanly,

204
00:12:12.820 --> 00:12:17.070
funds the operating layer, holds
the platform to its contract,

205
00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:21.737
gives every artifact an owner, and
staffs the desk from its measures.

206
00:12:21.737 --> 00:12:26.163
The CISO owns the verification ladder
and keeps automation read-only.

207
00:12:26.163 --> 00:12:29.181
The identity governance
officer reviews the desk's

208
00:12:29.181 --> 00:12:32.762
privileges and records a verdict
before any app is connected.

209
00:12:32.762 --> 00:12:38.437
System owners decide unknown assets and
close plan-of-action items only on re-test.

210
00:12:38.437 --> 00:12:40.937
The change board guards
the approval rules.

211
00:12:40.937 --> 00:12:43.526
The next step is free: run one reset,

212
00:12:43.526 --> 00:12:47.687
one joiner and one leaver,
and see what the record shows.

213
00:12:47.687 --> 00:12:50.037
Make the governed path the easy path.

214
00:12:50.037 --> 00:12:50.870
Thank you.
